{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A resumed run can execute a modified gate command or take the wrong phase, causing a failed or skipped check."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A credential matching part of the startup marker makes successful gates fail and blocks the run."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Overlapping credential matches in gate output can cause unbounded memory use and prolonged CPU consumption, preventing the factory from progressing."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"nuisance","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An unrelated object with matching fields gains a fabricated confinement field, with no broader compatibility failure demonstrated."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A downloaded patch containing literal terminal escape sequences is altered and no longer faithfully represents the stored change."},{"id":"C6","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"normal","reason":"Ordinary diagnostics containing an already-redacted credential lose their entire text, potentially hiding information needed to act on a failure."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A multiline credential split across stdout and stderr can be reconstructed and stored in gate results and logs."},{"id":"C8","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A fresh deployment process can expose a provider credential printed by a failing gate because its credential registry is empty."},{"id":"C9","consequence":"nuisance","trigger":"normal","reason":"The unused optional fields add metadata that existing consumers tolerate without changing their behavior."},{"id":"C4b","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Stripping meaningful escape bytes from a patch or request changes content presented to users or models."}]}